{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}What is a croissant? · Boulangerie{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article class="prose">
  <h1>What is a croissant?</h1>
  <p class="lede">A croissant is a small file that describes a dataset, a bit like the label on a jar. It says what's
    inside, where it came from, and how you're allowed to use it.</p>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>More and more research uses data from many places at once, often pulled in by software or AI tools rather than
    downloaded by hand. Those tools need a reliable way to answer simple questions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>What is this resource, and who made it?</li>
    <li>Can I reuse it, and how should I cite it?</li>
    <li>What files are there, and what's in each one?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A croissant answers these in a standard format, so your data is easier to find, gets used correctly, and gets cited properly.</p>

  <h2>Where does the name come from?</h2>
  <p><a href="https://mlcommons.org/croissant/">Croissant</a> is an open standard from MLCommons, a consortium of
    companies and universities working on machine learning. It builds on <a href="https://schema.org/Dataset">schema.org</a>,
    the vocabulary search engines already use to understand web pages.</p>

  <h2>What's in one?</h2>
  <dl class="layers">
    <dt>About your resource</dt><dd>Name, description, homepage, license, how to cite you, and any access rules.</dd>
    <dt>Your files</dt><dd>Each downloadable file: where it is, what kind of file it is, and how big it is.</dd>
    <dt>Your tables</dt><dd>For files that hold tables, the names of the columns and the kind of value in each.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Who made my croissant?</h2>
  <p>We drafted every croissant here automatically by reading each resource's public website and downloads. That
    means we may have got things wrong or missed things, which is why we're asking you to look. You know your
    resource best.</p>

  <h2>Do I have to do anything?</h2>
  <p>No. But if you have five minutes, please:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Find your resource on the <a href="{{ root }}index.html">list of croissants</a>.</li>
    <li>Read the "What your croissant says about you" section.</li>
    <li>If anything is wrong or missing, especially the license or how to cite you, use <strong>Suggest a correction</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Your suggestion becomes a proposed change that you can see before it's published. You'll need a free GitHub
    account to send it.</p>

  <h2>Is this official?</h2>
  <p>The croissant format is an official MLCommons standard. The croissants on this site are drafts made by RENCI to
    help biomedical knowledge bases get started. Once you're happy with yours, it's yours to publish and keep up to date.</p>
</article>
{% endblock %}
